for the telemtry slide
"This slide details Auto-PINN, the self-optimizing physics-informed neural network that corrects deformable mirror actuator drift in real time. Passive telemetry tapping captures Kalman filter innovation residuals and reconstruction-matrix condition numbers from the real-time loop without adding computational overhead. A causal transformer forecasts wind velocity and turbulence shifts using the last 50 frames, re-tuning the Kalman state matrix to prevent prediction decay. A Page-Hinkley statistical monitor detects actuator drift caused by thermal creep and faceplate fatigue. Once triggered, a MAML/Reptile meta-learning framework recalibrates the PINN's weights in under 60 seconds instead of hours of offline retraining. The updated challenger network is validated in shadow mode against live data, then promoted to champion via an atomic pointer swap with zero dropped frames. Tier 1 (real-time sensing) acts as the immediate pilot; Tiers 2 and 3 (background meta-learning) act as the flight controller that eliminates long-term calibration decay — this is the mechanism that targets σ²drift, the non-stationary actuator-response error term a static, offline-trained PINN cannot track."


Slide A — pairs with slide_requirement_coverage.png
On-slide caption (below/beside image):
"Every requirement in Challenge 9 — wavefront reconstruction, turbulence characterization, actuator mapping, and the 10ms real-time budget — is met by a specific, named component in our pipeline, not a generic claim."
Speaker notes:
"Our solution directly satisfies all four requirements of the Shack-Hartmann wavefront sensor challenge. Wavefront phase map reconstruction W(x,y) is handled by adaptive weighted-center-of-mass sub-pixel centroiding feeding a regularized modal and zonal reconstruction stage. Turbulence characterization via the Fried parameter r0 and coherence time tau0 is delivered through three independent methods: analytical estimation, a single-frame CNN estimator, and Cn2(h) altitude-resolved profiling. Deformable mirror actuator map derivation, compensating inter-actuator mechanical coupling, is solved with a linear H-inverse baseline corrected by our Auto-PINN physics-informed residual network. The strict real-time constraint is met by a C, BLAS, SIMD, and cuBLAS-accelerated control engine, profiled and validated to a worst-case p99.9 latency under 10 milliseconds per frame."


Slide B — pairs with slide_differentiation_usp.png
On-slide caption:
"How different is it, how does it solve the problem, and what's the USP — in one line: most designs calibrate once and let accuracy decay. Ours keeps recalibrating itself for the life of the mission, without breaking the real-time budget."
Speaker notes:
"This solution differs from a typical baseline implementation of this problem statement in five concrete ways. Where a standard pipeline calibrates its actuator correction once, offline, our Auto-PINN framework continuously self-optimizes its architecture, weights, and hyperparameters using automated machine learning. Where a standard pipeline estimates turbulence with a single analytical r0 and tau0 calculation, we combine analytical estimation, a CNN-based single-frame estimator, and Cn2(h) altitude profiling with transformer-based forecasting. Where a standard pipeline is purely reactive, reconstructing the wavefront and then correcting it, our Kalman and LQG predictive controller compensates for frame-to-actuation servo lag in advance. Where a standard pipeline's accuracy quietly degrades as the deformable mirror physically ages due to thermal creep and piezo hysteresis, our meta-learning system, using MAML and Reptile-style adaptation, recalibrates in under 60 seconds with zero dropped frames. Where a standard pipeline has no independent check on a machine learning corrector's output, we enforce an independent hard safety layer with stroke and rate limits and an uncertainty-weighted anomaly veto. The unique selling proposition of this solution is that it is the only architecture in this problem space where the actuator corrector keeps improving during the observing run instead of drifting out of calibration, and it does so inside the same sub-10-millisecond real-time budget required by the challenge."
